# Client OpenVPN configuration for Open Source Projects
# at the University of New Hampshire InterOperability Laboratory
# (UNH-IOL).
#
# This configuration may be used as-is with the Windows or Linux openvpn
# command line openvpn clients or TunnelBlick on Mac OS X (you may need
# to run openvpn with administrative privileges for the routes to be set
# up correctly once connected). Users of modern Linux distributions may
# import this file into NetworkManger. When using openvpn from the command
# line in Linux, DNS may fail to resolve. In those cases, you may need to 
# configure additonal if-up scripts within your system.
#
# Usage of this VPN connection is contingent upon agreement with the
# UNH-IOL Usage Agreement, available at the following URL:
# https://www.iol.unh.edu/sites/default/files/charters/unh-iol-usage-agreement.pdf
#
# You are expected to be physically present at your local system whenever
# it is connected to the VPN.
#
# The UNH-IOL Open Source VPN server is also set up to accept VPN connections
# over TCP, however, this will add overhead to your connection and will incur
# a performance hit.  It is recommended to only use TCP if your network 
# connection is preventing UDP for working.
#
# The UNH-IOL reserves the right to update or change the VPN configuration at 
# any time.  Where such changes may require the clients to obtain a new 
# configuration file.

client
dev tun
remote os-vpn.iol.unh.edu 1194 udp
resolv-retry infinite
nobind
persist-key
persist-tun
auth-user-pass
auth-nocache
remote-cert-tls server

cipher AES-256-GCM
verb 3
reneg-sec 86400
key-direction 1
auth SHA256

